I used two indexes here. `start` marks the beginning of a group, and `end` moves until the digit changes. This avoids special handling for the final group because every group is processed after `end` stops.

The input is not changed. The method only replaces the local string after a complete round.

```javascript
class Solution {
    countAndSay(n) {
        let current = "1";

        for (let round = 1; round < n; round++) {
            let next = "";
            let start = 0;

            while (start < current.length) {
                let end = start + 1;

                while (end < current.length && current[end] === current[start]) {
                    end++;
                }

                next += String(end - start) + current[start];
                start = end;
            }

            current = next;
        }

        return current;
    }
}
```

For the edge case `n = 1`, the loop does not run and the initial string is returned. Also, the comparison is for consecutive digits only, not for every occurrence of a digit in the whole string.

Time complexity: `O(L1 + L2 + ... + Ln)`, since every generated term is scanned once.

Space complexity: `O(Ln)` for the next term.

I kept the sequence generation iterative. Start with `"1"`, then read each consecutive group and write its count followed by the digit. The inner loop moves past a whole group, so the last group is handled in the same place as every other group.

For example, `"1211"` becomes `"111221"`: one `1`, one `2`, and two `1`s.

```cpp
class Solution {
  public:
    string countAndSay(int n) {
        string current = "1";

        for (int term = 1; term < n; term++) {
            string next;
            int index = 0;

            while (index < static_cast<int>(current.size())) {
                int start = index;
                char digit = current[index];

                while (index < static_cast<int>(current.size()) &&
                       current[index] == digit) {
                    index++;
                }

                next += to_string(index - start);
                next.push_back(digit);
            }

            current = next;
        }

        return current;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(L1 + L2 + ... + Ln)`, where `Li` is the length of term `i`. The final string length is the largest part of this work.

Space complexity: `O(Ln)` for the current and next strings.

The sequence contains digit characters, so a byte scan is enough. A `strings.Builder` makes it easy to append the count and the digit without repeatedly creating large temporary strings.

For `n = 5`, the terms are `1`, `11`, `21`, `1211`, and `111221`. Each round starts from the result of the previous round.

```go
import (
    "strconv"
    "strings"
)

func countAndSay(n int) string {
    current := "1"

    for term := 1; term < n; term++ {
        var next strings.Builder

        for index := 0; index < len(current); {
            digit := current[index]
            count := 0

            for index < len(current) && current[index] == digit {
                count++
                index++
            }

            next.WriteString(strconv.Itoa(count))
            next.WriteByte(digit)
        }

        current = next.String()
    }

    return current
}
```

The final group is counted by the inner loop because that loop stops either at a different byte or at the end of the string. No input array or string is modified.

Time complexity: `O(L1 + L2 + ... + Ln)`, based on scanning each term once.

Space complexity: `O(Ln)`, mainly for the generated strings.

The definition already gives a recursive form: term `n` is made by describing term `n - 1`. The helper scans one string from left to right and counts only adjacent equal characters.

A small dry run is `n = 3`. The call reaches `"1"`, changes it to `"11"`, and then reads `"11"` as two `1`s, giving `"21"`.

```java
class Solution {
    public String countAndSay(int n) {
        if (n == 1) {
            return "1";
        }

        return describe(countAndSay(n - 1));
    }

    private String describe(String text) {
        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        int index = 0;

        while (index < text.length()) {
            char digit = text.charAt(index);
            int count = 0;

            while (index < text.length() && text.charAt(index) == digit) {
                count++;
                index++;
            }

            result.append(count).append(digit);
        }

        return result.toString();
    }
}
```

The recursion depth is at most `n`. The important edge case is `n = 1`, which must return `"1"` without trying to read a previous term.

Time complexity: `O(L1 + L2 + ... + Ln)`, with `Li` representing the length of each generated term.

Space complexity: `O(Ln + n)`, for the strings and the recursive call stack.

## Intuition
Hey all, my intuition for this problem is very simple, just to find out for 'n', you must need at least n-1 String,
so deep down till n=2, make it with help of n-1, then pass it to the next n, hence you reached for the asked "n".

## Approach
I used very simple approach, just iterative, I have prevCompute for n=1, now compute n+1 from it, by using a helper function that iterate through the string, first count frequency till the current character match with prev character, the point it stop match, append freq and then the last digit after it make freq to zero and repeat till you reach last index.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public String countAndSay(int n) {
      if(n==1) return "1";

      String prevCompute="1";

      for(int i=2;i<=n;i++){
        String currCompute=countAndSayUtil(prevCompute);
        prevCompute=currCompute;
      }

      return prevCompute;
    }

    public String countAndSayUtil(String str){
        StringBuilder sb=new StringBuilder();

        int cnt=0,i=0;
        int n=str.length();

        while(i<n){
            int j=i;
            while(j<n && str.charAt(j)==str.charAt(i)){
                cnt+=1;
                j+=1;
            }
            sb.append((char)(cnt+'0'));
            sb.append(str.charAt(j-1));
            cnt=0;
            i=j;
        }

        return sb.toString();   
    }
}
```

